= BITwiki Constitution =

The Constitution records durable system invariants: principles that should survive changes in interface, model, extension stack, taxonomy, and implementation.

== Article I — Knowledge remains revisable ==
No page, agent output, model response, taxonomy, or implementation is infallible. Knowledge may be corrected as better evidence, stronger reasoning, clearer definitions, or more complete context becomes available. Revision history is part of the memory of the system.

== Article II — Evidence and provenance are first-class ==
The system must preserve the difference between a claim and its support. Where material claims require it, readers should be able to determine origin, evidence, assumptions/perspective, uncertainty, verification, and change history.

== Article III — Ontology, epistemology, and axiology remain distinct ==
Ontology asks what exists and how entities and relations are structured. Epistemology asks how knowledge is acquired, justified, interpreted, and validated. Axiology asks what is valued, prioritized, optimized, protected, or pursued. These dimensions interact recursively but must not be collapsed into one another.

== Article IV — Human agency is an invariant ==
Human–AI systems should increase human clarity, dignity, self-respect, and agency rather than manufacture dependence, compliance, coercion, or hidden loss of responsibility.

== Article V — Perspective may be plural; deception is not ==
BITwiki can contain multiple interpretations, schools, worldviews, models, and speculative frames. Pluralism does not license unsupported claims as established fact. Perspective should be explicit and reality-bound.

== Article VI — Knowledge should be transposable, modular, and composable ==
The system should preserve coherent canonical knowledge while allowing meaningful portions to be retrieved and reused independently. Modularity must preserve depth and nuance.

== Article VII — Public knowledge remains public; protected knowledge remains bounded ==
Publication is an explicit boundary transition. Public repositories and public wiki pages must not silently absorb private memory, secrets, protected identity data, credentials, or non-public operational material.

== Article VIII — Signal should compound ==
The system exists so valuable contribution can become reusable intelligence rather than disposable conversation.

== Article IX — Implementations serve the knowledge model ==
MediaWiki namespaces, categories, templates, Semantic MediaWiki properties, concepts, modules, extensions, portals, and interfaces are mechanisms. They may change. Intellectual distinctions and provenance should not be destroyed merely because implementation changes.

== Article X — The Constitution is versioned ==
Constitutional change must be deliberate and recoverable. Historical versions remain available through revision history or explicit archival provenance.

== Source lineage ==
Durable principles are recovered across the [[BITwiki:Manifesto|Manifesto]], [[BITwiki:Charter|Charter]], [[BITwiki:Foundational triad|Foundational triad]], public BIThub guidance, historical Book Matter work, and V1 governance/constitutional pages. The exact V1 [[BITwiki Constitution]] body remains an explicit reconciliation target.

